WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.953
Monitor the workload from user request to hardware.

00:00:03.953 --> 00:00:12.067
Track availability, errors, queue time, latency, and throughput alongside GPU utilization, VRAM, CPU, RAM, disk, and network use.

00:00:12.067 --> 00:00:19.765
Low utilization on an expensive accelerator is a signal to investigate data loading, runtime configuration, or demand.

00:00:19.765 --> 00:00:27.880
High VRAM use with allocation errors may require a smaller model, shorter context, different batch, or larger rental.

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:34.745
Health checks should prove that the model can answer, not merely that a process exists.

00:00:34.745 --> 00:00:41.195
For longer jobs, save checkpoints and outputs to durable storage on a defined schedule.

00:00:41.195 --> 00:00:45.564
Back up irreplaceable configuration and data, then test restoration.

00:00:45.564 --> 00:00:52.014
Do not rely on one instance disk as the only copy of completed work.

00:00:52.014 --> 00:00:58.464
Provider capacity can disappear between sessions, and interruptible machines may stop during a run.

00:00:58.464 --> 00:01:03.665
Maintain an acceptable alternate GPU type or region when deadlines matter.

00:01:03.665 --> 00:01:08.035
Design clients to handle transient failure and retries safely.

00:01:08.035 --> 00:01:14.900
Rebuild a replacement from documented scripts or images rather than repairing a unique machine indefinitely.

00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:18.437
A rental should be replaceable by design.

00:01:18.437 --> 00:01:22.390
Control change with versioned configurations and rollback plans.

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:29.672
A new driver, container, runtime, model revision, or quantization can change memory use, output, and performance.

00:01:29.672 --> 00:01:33.626
Test changes on limited work before adopting them.

00:01:33.626 --> 00:01:42.156
During an incident, isolate access, revoke credentials, preserve appropriate evidence, restore a known-good environment, and account for residual resources.

00:01:42.156 --> 00:01:53.183
Reliable rental operations mean the job can survive an instance loss and the team can end the session without losing results or leaving exposure behind.
